<%#
  Phone reminders, from the caregiver's side.

  The screen this replaces would have been a number field and a checkbox, and
  the checkbox is the whole problem: it would let one person arrange automated
  calls to another who had never agreed. So there is no control here that turns
  calls on. A caregiver may write down a number and ask it a question; only the
  person who answers can say yes, by pressing a key.

  The copy has to make that read as reasonable rather than obstructive, which is
  why the state line says what is true of the senior rather than what the
  caregiver is not allowed to do.
%>
<div class="bg-white overflow-hidden shadow rounded-lg">
  <div class="px-5 py-4 border-b border-gray-200 flex items-center justify-between">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-900">Phone reminders</h3>

    <% if senior.call_opted_out_at.present? %>
      <span class="inline-flex items-center px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-medium bg-gray-100 text-gray-800">Stopped</span>
    <% elsif senior.callable_by_phone? %>
      <span class="inline-flex items-center px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-medium bg-green-100 text-green-800">On</span>
    <% elsif senior.phone.present? %>
      <span class="inline-flex items-center px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-medium bg-yellow-100 text-yellow-800">Waiting for <%= senior.display_name %></span>
    <% else %>
      <span class="inline-flex items-center px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-medium bg-gray-100 text-gray-600">Not set up</span>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
    <% if senior.call_opted_out_at.present? %>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
        <%= senior.display_name %> asked us to stop calling on
        <%= (senior.local_time(at: senior.call_opted_out_at) || senior.call_opted_out_at).strftime("%B %-d") %>.
        We won't call again unless they say otherwise on a call they answer —
        that isn't something you or we can change for them.
      </p>

    <% elsif senior.callable_by_phone? %>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
        <%= senior.display_name %> agreed to phone reminders on
        <%= (senior.local_time(at: senior.call_consent_at) || senior.call_consent_at).strftime("%B %-d") %>.
        We'll call <strong><%= senior.phone %></strong> when a reminder is due, between
        <%= User::CALLING_HOURS.first %>am and <%= User::CALLING_HOURS.max + 1 - 12 %>pm their time.
        They can stop the calls at any point by pressing 9.
      </p>

    <% elsif senior.phone.present? %>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
        We have <strong><%= senior.phone %></strong> but haven't been told it's alright to use it.
        We'll ring once and ask <%= senior.display_name %> to press 1. Nothing is scheduled until they do.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
        Save this number in <%= senior.display_name %>'s phone first, as
        <strong>Remindly</strong> — otherwise the call shows up as an unknown
        number, and many phones send those straight to voicemail.
      </p>

    <% else %>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
        Remindly can telephone <%= senior.display_name %> when a reminder is due, and they
        press a key to say they've done it. Useful if they don't use a screen.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%#
      Not gated on the opt-out. The verification call is the only thing whose
      keypress can lift one, so hiding the button here made the stopped-state
      copy above — "unless they say otherwise on a call they answer" — a promise
      the screen itself made impossible to keep, reachable only by hand-crafting
      a POST. Asking again is permitted by design; the safeguards are that the
      senior still decides, that every attempt is recorded, and that the count
      sits under the button where a pattern of re-asking is visible.
    %>
    <% if permission == "manage" %>
      <%#
        The field is sized to its content, not to the page. An E.164 number is
        fifteen digits at most, and a full-width box invites the caregiver to
        wonder what else belongs in it. Sizing it also fixes what the stretched
        version did to the button: with the label and the hint inside the flex
        child, items-end aligned Save number to the bottom of all three, so it
        sat below the field it belongs to rather than beside it. The row now
        holds only the input and the button, and the hint sits under both.
      %>
      <%= form_with url: senior_phone_path(senior_id: senior.id), method: :patch do |f| %>
        <label for="user_phone" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-700">Their phone number</label>
        <div class="mt-1 flex items-center gap-3">
          <%= f.telephone_field :phone, value: senior.phone, name: "user[phone]", id: "user_phone",
                placeholder: "+15551234567",
                class: "w-56 border border-gray-300 rounded-md shadow-sm py-2 px-3 focus:ring-blue-500 focus:border-blue-500" %>
          <%= f.submit "Save number", class: "inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 border border-gray-300 text-sm font-medium rounded-md text-gray-700 bg-white hover:bg-gray-50 cursor-pointer" %>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-500">Include the country code. Changing it means asking again.</p>
      <% end %>

      <%#
        Which language the calls are spoken in. It sits with the number rather
        than on the senior's profile because the senior is often the one who
        never signs in — the same fact that makes the calls worth having.

        Only the telephone changes. The dashboard stays in English, which is
        what the hint says, because a caregiver reading "Language" on a settings
        panel will otherwise reasonably expect the screen to change too.
      %>
      <%= form_with url: senior_spoken_language_path(senior_id: senior.id), method: :patch do |f| %>
        <label for="user_spoken_language" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-700">Language for calls</label>
        <div class="mt-1 flex items-center gap-3">
          <%= f.select :spoken_language,
                options_for_select(User::SPOKEN_LANGUAGES.map { |code, meta| [ meta[:label], code ] }, senior.spoken_language),
                {},
                name: "user[spoken_language]", id: "user_spoken_language",
                class: "w-56 border border-gray-300 rounded-md shadow-sm py-2 px-3 focus:ring-blue-500 focus:border-blue-500" %>
          <%= f.submit "Save language", class: "inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 border border-gray-300 text-sm font-medium rounded-md text-gray-700 bg-white hover:bg-gray-50 cursor-pointer" %>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-500">
          What <%= senior.display_name %> hears on the phone. Remindly's screens stay in English.
        </p>
        <%# Remindly translates its own words, not the caregiver's. The reminder
            title is free text and goes to the voice exactly as typed, so a
            senior set to Spanish hears "Take meds" in the middle of a Spanish
            sentence unless somebody says this. Translating it for them would
            mean sending reminder titles to a third party — the same titles the
            forms ask people to keep clinical detail out of. %>
        <% unless senior.spoken_language == "en-US" %>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-amber-700">
            Write reminder titles in <%= senior.spoken_language_label.sub(/\s*\(.*\)/, "") %> too.
            Remindly speaks them exactly as you type them.
          </p>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>

      <%#
        Only while there is something to ask. Once they have agreed, this button
        would ring them to ask again — and the script it plays ends "press 9 and
        we won't call again", so its best outcome is a no-op and its worst is a
        senior switching off reminders that were working. A control whose only
        possible effect on a settled question is to unsettle it should not be on
        the screen.

        Still offered after an opt-out, deliberately: callable_by_phone? is false
        there, and that keypress is the only thing that can lift one.

        What belongs here instead, for a senior who has already agreed, is a test
        call — letting the caregiver hear what their mother will hear. That is
        written down in the design as step 5 and has never been built.
      %>
      <% if senior.phone.present? && !senior.callable_by_phone? %>
        <div class="pt-2 border-t border-gray-100">
          <%#
            The caregiver's own clock is not the one that governs this, and it
            is often not the one they are picturing -- the product assumes a
            daughter some hours from her mother. verify_phone says the local
            time too, but only once the button has been pressed and only when
            the guard fires, so it cannot help with the case this line exists
            for: a tz that resolves cleanly and is simply wrong reads as
            permission, and the guard stays quiet. Nobody but the caregiver
            looking at this screen can tell that "10:30am" is nonsense for a
            number they dial themselves, so the clock has to be in front of
            them before they act, not after they are refused.
          %>
          <% local_now = senior.local_time %>
          <% may_call = senior.within_calling_hours? %>

          <% if local_now %>
            <p class="mb-3 text-sm <%= may_call ? "text-gray-600" : "text-amber-700" %>">
              It's <strong><%= local_now.strftime("%-l:%M%P") %> (<%= local_now.strftime("%Z") %>)</strong>
              for <%= senior.display_name %>.
              <% unless may_call %>
                We only call between <%= User::CALLING_HOURS.first %>am and
                <%= User::CALLING_HOURS.max + 1 - 12 %>pm, so this won't ring yet.
              <% end %>
            </p>
          <% elsif !may_call %>
            <%#
              The button is disabled below, and a disabled control with nothing
              explaining it is worse than no control. An unresolvable zone is the
              other way within_calling_hours? says no, and it is the caregiver who
              can fix it — so it has to be named rather than left to look broken.
            %>
            <p class="mb-3 text-sm text-amber-700">
              We can't tell what time it is where <%= senior.display_name %> is —
              their timezone is set to <strong><%= senior.tz.presence || "nothing" %></strong>,
              which isn't one we recognise. We won't call until that's corrected.
            </p>
          <% end %>

          <%#
            Disabled outside calling hours rather than left to fail on submit.
            The controller still refuses -- that guard is what actually protects
            the senior, and it stays -- but a button that round-trips to an alert
            repeating the sentence directly above it is the same defect this file
            objects to a few lines down, where the attempt count is kept honest
            precisely so the screen never offers what the model will refuse.
          %>
          <%= button_to "Call and ask #{senior.display_name}",
                verify_senior_phone_path(senior_id: senior.id),
                method: :post,
                disabled: !may_call,
                class: "inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 border border-transparent text-sm font-medium rounded-md text-white #{may_call ? "bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700" : "bg-gray-300 cursor-not-allowed"}" %>
          <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-gray-500">
            <%#
              "in the last day" is literal: verifications_in_window counts a
              rolling 24 hours from now, per number. An earlier version of this
              comment said "per UTC day", which is what the row's call_day stamp
              records for the uniqueness index — but the *bound* has never been
              the calendar day, and describing it as one would have the next
              person expecting the allowance to refill at midnight. The count is
              clamped because a screen offering a negative
              number of attempts has clearly lost track of something.
            %>
            <% if senior.call_opted_out_at.present? %>
              <%= senior.display_name %> has asked us to stop, so this only asks again —
              it cannot undo that. Nothing changes unless they press 1 themselves.
            <% end %>
            <% if attempts.any? %>
              Asked <%= pluralize(attempts.size, "time") %> in the last day<%= ", most recently #{time_ago_in_words(attempts.last.created_at)} ago" if attempts.last.created_at %>.
              <%= pluralize([ TelnyxCall::MAX_VERIFICATIONS_PER_DAY - attempts.size, 0 ].max, "attempt") %> left.
            <% elsif senior.call_opted_out_at.blank? %>
              We'll ring once. If nobody answers, or they'd rather not, nothing changes.
            <% end %>
          </p>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    <% elsif permission != "manage" %>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">You have view-only access, so you can't change this.</p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
